*We denote (\textbackslash{}beta\_\{m\} = (\textbackslash{}lambda\_\{m\},\textbackslash{}omega\_\{m\},\textbackslash{}eta\_\{m\},\textbackslash{}epsilon\_\{m\})) as the sampling parameters for domain (m) .We use a format of sigmoid to ensure the sampling value is monotonically decreasing as the quality rank goes up (worse quality) and (\textbackslash{}lambda\_\{m\}) is used to adjust how fast it decreases. (\textbackslash{}omega\_\{m\}) controls the quality percentile threshold, determining the minimum quality level we aim to retain. (\textbackslash{}eta\_\{m\}) is a scaling parameter that adjusts the sampling values, while (\textbackslash{}epsilon\_\{m\}) introduces randomness to incorporate data from all quality ranges. By applying different sampling parameters across domains, we achieve flexible control over domain proportions.
